  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does printing to PDF remove metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "No. Printing to PDF does not remove metadata — it replaces the original document's metadata with new metadata generated by the PDF print driver. The resulting file gets a new Author field (from your OS user account), a new CreationDate (the moment you printed), a new Producer field identifying the print driver, and a new XMP stream with all of these fields duplicated. The original document's Author, edit history, and keyword fields are lost — but replaced with your current user identity and a fresh timestamp, which may be equally or more revealing."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What metadata does print to PDF create?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Print to PDF creates a fresh set of metadata from the operating system and print driver: Author (your Windows or macOS username), CreationDate (current date and time), Producer (the print driver name — e.g. 'Microsoft Print to PDF' or 'macOS PDF filter'), and Creator (the application you printed from). It also creates an XMP stream with all of these fields. The original document's revision history, internal keywords, and manager field are not carried over — but you get a new, clearly dated document bearing your name."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does saving as PDF remove metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "No. Saving or exporting as PDF from Word, Google Docs, or any authoring application preserves the document's existing metadata — Author, Creator, Keywords, revision history — and adds a new ModDate timestamp. It does not remove metadata. The only methods that actually remove metadata are dedicated stripping tools: ExifTool (command line, free), Adobe Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document function, or browser-based tools like FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover which removes both the Info dictionary and XMP stream."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does print to PDF remove author?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "No. Print to PDF removes the original document's Author field but immediately replaces it with your current OS username as the new Author. If you print from Windows, the Author in the resulting PDF is your Windows account name. On macOS it is your system account name. The result is a PDF that still has an Author field — just a different one. The only way to remove the Author field entirely is to use a dedicated metadata removal tool after printing."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I actually remove metadata from a PDF?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The correct methods are: (1) ExifTool command line — run 'exiftool -all= document.pdf' to strip both the Info dictionary and XMP stream; (2) FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover at fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover — browser-based, removes both stores, nothing uploaded; (3) Adobe Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document function — Tools > Redact > Sanitize Document. Printing to PDF, re-saving, and changing the file extension do not remove metadata."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why does printing to PDF not remove metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "When you print to PDF, the operating system's PDF print driver renders the document as a visual output and generates a new PDF file from scratch. This new file gets metadata from the print driver itself — your OS username becomes the Author, the print timestamp becomes the CreationDate, and the driver identifies itself as the Producer. Metadata removal is not a function of PDF generation — it requires a separate, explicit stripping operation on the output file."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

