    <h2 id="what-actually-works">#4. What actually removes metadata</h2>

    <p>Three methods reliably remove metadata from both the Info dictionary and the XMP stream — the two places where your name and timestamps live in a modern PDF.</p>

    <h3>Method 1 — ExifTool (free, command line)</h3>

    <div class="code-block">
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
      <span class="code-label">Remove all metadata — Info dictionary + XMP stream</span>
      <span class="c-comment"># Install: brew install exiftool (Mac) | apt install libimage-exiftool-perl (Linux)</span><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># Windows: exiftool.org/install.html</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-arg">-all= -overwrite_original document.pdf</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-comment"># Verify both stores are cleared</span><br>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-arg">-Author -Creator -Producer -XMP:all document.pdf</span><br>
      <span class="c-ok"># No output = fully clean</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Method 2 — FileIntel PDF Metadata Remover (free, browser)</h3>

    <p>Drop your PDF into <a href="/pdf-metadata-remover">fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover</a>. The tool removes the Info dictionary, trailer dictionary entries, and XMP stream in a single pass. Files are processed locally — nothing transmitted to any server. The Privacy Risk Score shows every field before and after stripping so you can confirm what was removed.</p>

    <h3>Method 3 — Adobe Acrobat Pro Sanitize Document</h3>

    <p>Tools &rarr; Redact &rarr; Sanitize Document. This performs a complete PDF rewrite — the most thorough approach, addressing metadata, embedded objects, and incremental update history. More thorough than ExifTool for complex documents. Always verify with ExifTool after, as behaviour varies across Acrobat versions.</p>

    <div class="callout green">
      <div class="callout-label">&#10003; The correct workflow</div>
      <p>The right sequence is: export your document to PDF normally from Word or whatever application you use, then run it through a dedicated metadata stripping tool before sending. Not: export → print to PDF → send. The print-to-PDF step adds a process layer that doesn't clean anything and timestamps the processing.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="when-print-to-pdf-is-useful">#5. When print to PDF is actually useful for privacy</h2>

    <p>Print to PDF is not useless for privacy purposes. It just serves a different purpose than metadata removal.</p>

    <p><strong>Removing revision history.</strong> Print to PDF loses the edit history embedded in the document structure. A Word document with 47 revision saves produces a print-to-PDF output with no revision count. If the RevisionNumber revealing your edit volume is the specific concern, print to PDF addresses it — though ExifTool does too, more cleanly.</p>

    <p><strong>Removing embedded macros and scripts.</strong> Print to PDF produces a static visual output. Active content — macros, embedded JavaScript, linked data sources — doesn't survive the render. For documents where you're concerned about active content reaching the recipient, print to PDF is more effective than a metadata strip alone.</p>

    <p><strong>Removing complex embedded objects.</strong> OLE objects, linked spreadsheets, embedded audio, and similar content don't survive print to PDF. The output is a static page image. If the concern is complex embedded content rather than standard metadata, print to PDF helps.</p>

    <p>In all three cases, the right workflow is: print to PDF first, then strip the resulting PDF's metadata with ExifTool or FileIntel. You get the benefits of print-to-PDF (no revision history, no active content) combined with proper metadata removal. Not one or the other.</p>

    <h2 id="verifying-your-output">#6. How to verify your document is actually clean</h2>

    <p>After any cleaning method, run this single ExifTool command to check both stores simultaneously:</p>

    <div class="code-block">
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
      <span class="code-label">Complete verification — both Info dictionary and XMP stream</span>
      <span class="c-prompt">$ </span><span class="c-cmd">exiftool</span> <span class="c-arg">-Author -Manager -Keywords -Creator -Producer -CreateDate -ModifyDate -XMP:all document.pdf</span><br><br>
      <span class="c-ok"># If this returns NO output at all — the document is clean</span><br>
      <span class="c-warn"># If anything is returned — those fields still contain data</span>
    </div>

    <p>The most common verification mistake is only checking Acrobat's Properties panel. That panel shows the Info dictionary — it does not show the XMP stream. A document can appear blank in Acrobat Properties while still carrying your full name, original creation date, and software version in the XMP store. ExifTool shows both. For sensitive documents, always check both.</p>

    <p>For a complete technical breakdown of what the XMP stream contains and how to specifically target it, see our guide on <a href="/blog/remove-xmp-metadata-pdf">removing XMP metadata from a PDF</a>. For the full metadata removal workflow covering every method and every metadata layer, see <a href="/blog/remove-metadata-from-pdf">how to remove metadata from a PDF</a>.</p>

