        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">No. Printing to PDF replaces the original document's metadata with new metadata from the print driver. The resulting PDF contains your OS username as Author, the current date and time as CreationDate, the print driver name as Producer, and an XMP stream duplicating all of these. Some original fields like revision count and internal keywords may not carry over — but your name and a fresh timestamp always do.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">What metadata does print to PDF create?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Print to PDF generates: Author (your Windows or macOS username), CreationDate (the exact moment you printed), Producer (the print driver name — e.g. "Microsoft Print to PDF" or "macOS PDF filter"), Creator (the application you printed from), and an XMP stream duplicating all of these fields. These are supplied by the operating system and print driver, not the original document.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Does saving as PDF remove metadata?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">No. Saving or exporting as PDF from Word, Google Docs, or any authoring application preserves all existing metadata and adds a new ModDate. Author, Manager, Keywords, revision count — all carried over. To remove metadata, use a dedicated stripping tool after the export: ExifTool (<code>exiftool -all= document.pdf</code>), FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover (browser, free), or Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document function.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Does print to PDF remove author?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">No. Print to PDF removes the original document's Author field but immediately replaces it with your OS username. On Windows, that is your Windows account name. On macOS, it is your system account name. The resulting PDF has an Author field — just a different one sourced from the operating system rather than the document. To remove the Author field entirely, strip the PDF's metadata after printing using ExifTool or a dedicated tool.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">Why does printing to PDF not remove metadata?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">When you print to PDF, the print driver renders the document as a visual output and generates a new PDF file. This new file gets metadata from the print driver and the operating system — your username, the current timestamp, the driver name. Metadata removal is not part of the PDF generation process. It requires a separate, explicit stripping operation on the output file using a dedicated tool like ExifTool.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q">How do I actually remove metadata from a PDF?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-a"><div class="faq-a-inner">Three methods work reliably: (1) ExifTool — <code>exiftool -all= document.pdf</code> removes both the Info dictionary and XMP stream; (2) FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover at fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover — browser-based, removes both stores, nothing uploaded; (3) Adobe Acrobat Pro's Sanitize Document — Tools > Redact > Sanitize Document. After any method, verify with <code>exiftool -XMP:all document.pdf</code> to confirm the XMP stream was cleared.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="legal-note">This article describes how PDF print drivers handle metadata based on observable ExifTool output across common operating systems and applications. Behaviour may vary across OS versions, print driver versions, and application configurations. Always verify metadata removal with ExifTool on the specific output file before sharing sensitive documents.</p>

